            <ArticleTitle>Headache and the primary empty sella syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>883-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Otolaryngologists frequently encounter patients with complaints of headaches. On occasion, normal physical examination findings may be accompanied by a roentgenographic enlargement of the sella turcica. In the process of evaluation for an intrasellar neoplasm, an &quot;empty sella&quot; is occasionally discovered. Patients with this condition commonly have headaches, although a cause-effect relationship is controversial. Endocrine and visual disturbances, although rare necessitate periodic evaluation of these patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
